From 1247d09e4ed33d70cdab7de52e4082b116db2728 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:46:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 41/48] vrend: share an exported texture through host memory

A Venus context can only import a resource the render server can receive:
a dma-buf, or a shared-memory segment. An Apple host has neither GBM nor
dma-buf, and a vrend texture's storage is a GL object with no fd at all, so
proxy_context_attach_resource() reported "exported res N to unexpected
fd_type -1" for every gralloc buffer an Android guest handed to its Vulkan
driver, and vkr answered every vkGetMemoryResourcePropertiesMESA with
"invalid res_id".

The renderer already shares memory the other way round: a Venus context
exports a blob as a segment and vrend_resource_import_shm() maps it and
refreshes a texture from it before each read. Give the export direction the
same shape. When a resource with no fd of its own is asked to export one --
which happens only when another context is importing it -- allocate a host
segment the size of the texture's tightly packed linear layout, snapshot the
texture into it, and hand out its fd as VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_SHM. The segment
becomes the resource's shared storage, recorded in the same shm_import_*
fields an imported blob uses, so a single refresh path serves both
directions and both pin the segment through the same helper. The proxy then
carries such an export to the render server, which already imports a
segment; only its dma-buf-only check stood in the way, and the size it takes
from the exported descriptor now falls back to the size the file reports,
because a shared-memory object need not be seekable and on this host is not.

The stride is width * blocksize, with no padding: that is the layout the
guest allocator computed for the same width and format when it asked for the
buffer, and the layout the importing driver is told about.

Synchronisation points, in both directions:

  - Importer to GL. The texture is refreshed from the segment before every
    GL read of the resource -- when it is bound through a sampler view and
    when it is the source of a blit or copy -- which are the points the
    import direction already refreshes at.
  - GL to importer. Binding the resource as a colour attachment marks it
    dirty; the segment is rewritten from the texture at the end of the
    command submission that drew into it, and again before the resource's fd
    is handed to another context. The guest fences a submission before it
    passes a buffer on, so that fence is the earliest point another consumer
    can look at the pixels.

Neither direction adds a host-side fence: ordering comes from the guest,
exactly as it does for an imported blob. A buffer that is never exported
keeps ordinary texture storage and an unchanged code path, and the whole
export conversion is Metal-host only, so a host with dma-buf keeps its GBM
export untouched.
